 PK4.Personal Care.1 (Self-Care Skills): Student demonstrates independence in personal care and daily living activities (e.g., dressing, opening food containers, managing clothing fasteners).",Early Childhood Fine Motor & Self-Care Skill Development,"The ""Little Hands, Big Future"" project builds essential hand strength, finger coordination, and self-care independence in early childhood learners through hands-on, play-based motor stations. Post-pandemic early learners increasingly enter preschool with significant delays in manual dexterity, making everyday tasks like holding a pencil, using scissors, buttoning a coat, or opening a milk carton at lunch difficult and frustrating.
 
 This proposal establishes a multi-sensory Fine Motor and Self-Care Lab in the classroom. Funding will provide specialized tools including loop scissors, jumbo tweezers, resistance putty, lock-and-key sets, button/zipper dressing boards, and food-container practice kits (milk carton simulators, twist-cap jars, and pouch openers). Activities run daily during small-group centers over a 36-week school year. Students rotate through targeted stations: pinching pom-poms with tongs, twisting locks, practicing daily life fasteners on dressing frames, and exercising hand muscles with weighted therapy clay.
 
 What makes this project innovative is its dual focus: blending therapeutic motor exercises with practical real-world self-care skills. Rather than isolated handwriting drills, children build the palm muscles needed for writing while learning how to open their own lunch items and dress themselves for recess. This project bridges the gap between physical readiness, personal independence, and kindergarten success.",500,18,"Project success will be measured using a multi-step pre- and post-assessment framework evaluated quarterly:
 
